course: dictionary
lesson: 7190
title: Twisting the truth
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: To pervert something means to change it so it is used wrongly or for a bad purpose. It is a verb, so it is something you do. «Do not pervert justice or show partiality.» Justice means fair treatment for everyone, so to pervert justice means to twist it so it is no longer fair.
explain: Context helps again. Here the sentence warns judges. Judges should step back when a friend takes part in a case, because a friend could make them unfair. This is called a conflict of interest. Ask yourself: what would be a bad way to twist justice? Lying or hiding evidence would be bad, so pervert must mean to spoil something that should be fair.
explain: In law, «pervert the course of justice» is a serious crime. It means trying to stop the police or the courts from finding the truth, for example by lying or hiding evidence. This crime is taken very seriously in the UK, Ireland and Australia.
fact: To pervert something means to change it so it is used wrongly or for a bad purpose.
fact: «Pervert the course of justice» means trying to stop the police or courts finding the truth.
fact: Hiding evidence or telling lies to the police can be a crime.
fact: «Partiality» means being unfair, by favouring one side.
fact: A conflict of interest is when a person's own connections could make them unfair.
exercise: «Do not pervert justice or show partiality.» What does pervert mean here? | To make something clean and new | To hurry through a job | To write a long letter | *To twist something so it is used wrongly
exercise: Which action would pervert the course of justice? | *Hiding evidence from the police | Helping an old lady cross the road | Handing in a lost purse | Telling the police what you saw
exercise: Which word in «Do not pervert justice or show partiality» names the thing that should be fair? | *justice | pervert | not | show
test: «Judges should step back when a friend takes part in a case.» Why? | Judges like to be alone | Friends are never allowed to talk in court | The judge is tired | *A friend could make the judge unfair
test: In «pervert the truth», what does pervert mean? | To write the truth in pencil | *To twist the truth so it is misleading | To tell the truth loudly | To forget the truth
exam: Which sentence uses pervert correctly? | The guard tried to pervert a friendly wave | The guard tried to pervert a cake by eating it | *The guard tried to pervert the course of justice by hiding the evidence | The guard tried to pervert a sunny day
